-- pending: #58 (an implicit copy of an `imm` borrow, here one read at each turn of a loop; SPEC §42 Open)
-- now: 20:19: `b` is used twice here, and its type `imm 'x i32` is not persistent
--
-- A borrow block whose body loops, with a local of the block named as a function: the local
-- does not stay in scope after the block, so `k()` after it is the declared `k` (hygiene, #481).
--
-- The lend spans the loop's ticks, which the kernel admits at the graded row `div call`
-- (SPEC §48). The loop then reads the borrow `b` at each turn, so its state threads it and
-- each turn uses it again; a borrow is not persistent, and an `imm` borrow is not copied
-- implicitly (#58, SPEC §42 Open), so the checker refuses the second use, as it does
-- `corpus/reject/lend-iter-borrow.bc`'s.
--
-- Once it lands: `i32[207]`, `live 0`: the loop's 2, times 100, plus the declared `k`, 7.
fn k() -> i32 = 7
main -> i32 =
  let t = alloc(1);
  let r = borrow imm t { 'x, b ->
    let k: !(1 => i32) = !{ u -> 1 };
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < 2 { i = i + *b };
    i
  };
  let v = free(t);
  r * 100 + k()
